## Runtime Borrow Checking with RefCell

`RefCell[T]` enables interior mutability for non-Copy types. Unlike Cell, it enforces borrow rules at **runtime** instead of compile time.

### Creating and Using RefCell

```xiom
let cell = RefCell.new(Vec[Int]());

// Borrow mutably
{
  let mut_ref = cell.borrow_mut();
  mut_ref.push(1);
  mut_ref.push(2);
}  // borrow ends

// Borrow immutably
let ref = cell.borrow();
let len = ref.len();  // 2
```

### borrow vs borrow_mut

- `.borrow()` returns a shared reference (`&T`) — multiple allowed
- `.borrow_mut()` returns a mutable reference (`&mut T`) — exclusive

### Runtime Enforcement

RefCell checks borrow rules at runtime — if violated, it **traps** (panics):

```xiom
let cell = RefCell.new(42);

let r1 = cell.borrow();
let r2 = cell.borrow();  // OK — multiple shared
// let m = cell.borrow_mut();  // TRAP: already borrowed!
```

### RefCell with Rc

```xiom
let shared = Rc.new(RefCell.new(Vec[Int]()));

let clone = shared.clone();
shared.borrow_mut().push(1);
clone.borrow_mut().push(2);
// Both see the same Vec with [1, 2]
```

### RefCell vs Cell

| Feature | Cell | RefCell |
|---------|------|---------|
| Type constraint | Copy types only | Any type |
| Access method | get/set (copy) | borrow/borrow_mut (ref) |
| Borrow checking | Not needed | Runtime |
| Overhead | None | Reference counting |

### When to Use RefCell

- Shared mutable non-Copy data (Vec, Map, custom structs)
- Implementing complex data structures (graphs, doubly-linked lists)
- When compile-time borrow checking is too restrictive

